<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Insect Identification Guide</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
  </head>
  <body>

    <header>
      <div class="header-label">Entomology Field Guide</div>
      <h1>Insect <em>Identification</em> Key</h1>
      <p>Click the choices below to navigate the dichotomous key and identify your specimen.</p>
    </header>

    <main>
      <div class="key-container">

        <div class="step-card" id="step-1">
          <div class="step-header">Step 1: Wing Check</div>
          <p class="question">Does your insect specimen have visible wings?</p>

          <div class="choices-layout">
            <a href="#step-3" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">A</span> Yes, wings are visible (or tucked under covers)
            </a>

            <a href="#step-2" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">B</span> No, it is completely wingless
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="step-card" id="step-2">
          <div class="step-header">Step 2: Wingless Profiles</div>
          <p class="question">Look closely at the body shape and antennae:</p>

          <div class="choices-layout">
            <input type="checkbox" id="trigger-hymenoptera" class="click-gate">
            <label for="trigger-hymenoptera" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">A</span> Distinct narrow waist, or elbowed antennae
            </label>
            <div class="result-box text-hymenoptera">
              <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/59" target="_blank" class="bugguide-link">
                <h3>💥 Result: Order Hymenoptera (Ants / Wingless Wasps) 🌐</h3>
              </a>
              <p>Ants have a highly distinct pinched waist with nodes. Worker ants are entirely wingless but belong to this highly social order.</p>
              <label for="trigger-hymenoptera" class="btn-reset">🔄 Clear Selection</label>
            </div>

            <input type="checkbox" id="trigger-blattodea-wingless" class="click-gate">
            <label for="trigger-blattodea-wingless" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">B</span> Broad flat body, long thread-like antennae
            </label>
            <div class="result-box text-blattodea">
              <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/342386" target="_blank" class="bugguide-link">
                <h3>💥 Result: Order Blattodea (Cockroaches / Termites) 🌐</h3>
              </a>
              <p>Wingless roaches and termites have flat, oval-shaped bodies built for crawling through tight spaces and leaf litter.</p>
              <label for="trigger-blattodea-wingless" class="btn-reset">🔄 Clear Selection</label>
            </div>
          </div>
          <a href="#step-1" class="back-link">← Back to Step 1</a>
        </div>

        <div class="step-card" id="step-3">
          <div class="step-header">Step 3: Wing Count</div>
          <p class="question">How many individual wings or pairs can you see?</p>

          <div class="choices-layout">
            <input type="checkbox" id="trigger-diptera" class="click-gate">
            <label for="trigger-diptera" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">A</span> Only 1 pair of clear wings (plus 2 tiny balance knobs)
            </label>
            <div class="result-box text-diptera">
              <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/51" target="_blank" class="bugguide-link">
                <h3>💥 Result: Order Diptera (True Flies) 🌐</h3>
              </a>
              <p>True flies unique trait is having only two wings. Their hind wings shrunk over millions of years into small flight stabilizers called halteres.</p>
              <label for="trigger-diptera" class="btn-reset">🔄 Clear Selection</label>
            </div>

            <a href="#step-4" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">B</span> 2 pairs of wings (4 wings total)
            </a>
          </div>
          <a href="#step-1" class="back-link">← Back to Step 1</a>
        </div>

        <div class="step-card" id="step-4">
          <div class="step-header">Step 4: Wing Texture</div>
          <p class="question">Examine the texture of the outer front wings:</p>

          <div class="choices-layout">
            <input type="checkbox" id="trigger-coleoptera" class="click-gate">
            <label for="trigger-coleoptera" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">A</span> Hard, shell-like covers meeting in a straight line down the back
            </label>
            <div class="result-box text-coleoptera">
              <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/60" target="_blank" class="bugguide-link">
                <h3>💥 Result: Order Coleoptera (Beetles) 🌐</h3>
              </a>
              <p>Beetles modify their front wings into hard shields called elytra. They protect the delicate flight wings folded underneath them.</p>
              <label for="trigger-coleoptera" class="btn-reset">🔄 Clear Selection</label>
            </div>

            <input type="checkbox" id="trigger-orthoptera" class="click-gate">
            <label for="trigger-orthoptera" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">B</span> Leathery, straight front wings & large jumping hind legs
            </label>
            <div class="result-box text-orthoptera">
              <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/73" target="_blank" class="bugguide-link">
                <h3>💥 Result: Order Orthoptera (Grasshoppers & Crickets) 🌐</h3>
              </a>
              <p>Orthoptera means "straight wings." Their leathery forewings cover delicate fan-folding wings optimized for hopping escapes.</p>
              <label for="trigger-orthoptera" class="btn-reset">🔄 Clear Selection</label>
            </div>

            <a href="#step-5" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">C</span> Clear, thin, or colorful powdery wings
            </a>
          </div>
          <a href="#step-3" class="back-link">← Back to Step 3</a>
        </div>

        <div class="step-card" id="step-5">
          <div class="step-header">Step 5: Wing Surfaces</div>
          <p class="question">Are the wings clear or covered in colorful dust?</p>

          <div class="choices-layout">
            <input type="checkbox" id="trigger-lepidoptera" class="click-gate">
            <label for="trigger-lepidoptera" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">A</span> Covered in dusty, overlapping micro-scales
            </label>
            <div class="result-box text-lepidoptera">
              <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/57" target="_blank" class="bugguide-link">
                <h3>💥 Result: Order Lepidoptera (Butterflies & Moths) 🌐</h3>
              </a>
              <p>The colored dust on a butterfly's wing is actually thousands of tiny scales that insulate the insect and generate vivid patterns.</p>
              <label for="trigger-lepidoptera" class="btn-reset">🔄 Clear Selection</label>
            </div>

            <input type="checkbox" id="trigger-odonata" class="click-gate">
            <label for="trigger-odonata" class="btn-choice">
              <span class="letter">B</span> Completely transparent with a heavy mesh grid of veins
            </label>
            <div class="result-box text-odonata">
              <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/77" target="_blank" class="bugguide-link">
                <h3>💥 Result: Order Odonata (Dragonflies & Damselflies) 🌐</h3>
              </a>
              <p>Ancient aviators with glass-like wings containing complex support networks. Excellent predatory speed flyers.</p>
              <label for="trigger-odonata" class="btn-reset">🔄 Clear Selection</label>
            </div>
          </div>
          <a href="#step-4" class="back-link">← Back to Step 4</a>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="orders-gallery-section">
        <h2 class="gallery-title">All Insect Orders Covered By This Key</h2>
        <p class="gallery-subtitle">A quick-reference catalog of the key anatomical groups (Click any card to explore BugGuide).</p>

        <div class="orders-grid">

          <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/60" target="_blank" class="catalog-card-link">
            <div class="catalog-card">
              <div class="catalog-badge">🪲</div>
              <div class="catalog-content">
                <h3>Coleoptera</h3>
                <span class="common-name">Beetles</span>
                <p>The largest insect order on planet Earth. Recognized by hard protective shell-wings (elytra) that form a straight seam line perfectly down the spine.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </a>

          <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/57" target="_blank" class="catalog-card-link">
            <div class="catalog-card">
              <div class="catalog-badge">🦋</div>
              <div class="catalog-content">
                <h3>Lepidoptera</h3>
                <span class="common-name">Butterflies & Moths</span>
                <p>Characterized by four broad wings completely covered in thousands of colorful, dust-like microscopic scales. Often have a rolled feeding tube mouth.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </a>

          <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/51" target="_blank" class="catalog-card-link">
            <div class="catalog-card">
              <div class="catalog-badge">🪰</div>
              <div class="catalog-content">
                <h3>Diptera</h3>
                <span class="common-name">True Flies & Mosquitoes</span>
                <p>Uniquely equipped with only two functional flight wings. Their hind wings shrunk over time into two little clubbed balance anchors called halteres.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </a>

          <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/59" target="_blank" class="catalog-card-link">
            <div class="catalog-card">
              <div class="catalog-badge">🐝</div>
              <div class="catalog-content">
                <h3>Hymenoptera</h3>
                <span class="common-name">Bees, Wasps & Ants</span>
                <p>Includes species with clear, dynamic hooked wing sets or completely wingless varieties (like workers). Best identified by an exceptionally narrow body waist.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </a>

          <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/73" target="_blank" class="catalog-card-link">
            <div class="catalog-card">
              <div class="catalog-badge">🦗</div>
              <div class="catalog-content">
                <h3>Orthoptera</h3>
                <span class="common-name">Grasshoppers & Crickets</span>
                <p>Famous for muscular, elongated rear leg configurations tuned entirely for jumping. Have straight, leathery front armor wings shielding fan-like flight wings.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </a>

          <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/77" target="_blank" class="catalog-card-link">
            <div class="catalog-card">
              <div class="catalog-badge">🐉</div>
              <div class="catalog-content">
                <h3>Odonata</h3>
                <span class="common-name">Dragonflies & Damselflies</span>
                <p>Stunning aerial predators with giant compound eyes covering their heads. Supported by two pairs of elongated, intricately net-veined transparent wings.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </a>

          <a href="https://bugguide.net/node/view/342386" target="_blank" class="catalog-card-link">
            <div class="catalog-card">
              <div class="catalog-badge">🪳</div>
              <div class="catalog-content">
                <h3>Blattodea</h3>
                <span class="common-name">Cockroaches & Termites</span>
                <p>Flat, low-profile oval shapes designed for crawling into leaf litter. Equipped with long, highly dynamic whip antennae and swift running legs.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </a>

        </div>
      </div>
    </main>

    <footer>
      Dichotomous System Guide — Developed for Code.org Web Lab
    </footer>

  </body>
</html>
